{"t":1789495894,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"0dc077dc-7102-45c4-b0a3-d5f990e30798","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-15T13:29:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 151329\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n929 AM EDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNo changes have been made to the forecast this morning.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Remaining cool today, with shower and thunderstorm chances\n  returning late in the week and into the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Remaining cool today, with shower and \nthunderstorm chances returning late in the week and into the \nweekend.\n\nWith a broad 1026-mb surface anticyclone anchored over the \nnortheastern U.S., expect conditions to be fairly similar to \nyesterday/Monday. The one major difference will be the marked \nreduction in temperatures and humidity, as well as winds given \nweak gradients within the influence of this dome of high \npressure. Today's high temperatures are forecast to reach the \nmid 70s to low 80s (upper 60s to low 70s in the mountains). This\ncomes with continued low humidity with dew points mainly in the\nupper 40s to low 50s. Aside from some passing high cirrus, \nabundant sunshine is expected throughout the day. Light \nsoutheasterly gradients will favor an uptick in overnight \ntemperatures. While still cooler than recent days, most can \nanticipate lows in the mid 50s to low 60s (mildest along the \nI-95 urban areas as well as near the Potomac River and \nChesapeake Bay shorelines).\n\nBehind an exiting longwave trough that pushes into the Canadian\nMaritimes, mid/upper heights begin to build in earnest. An\nexpansive near 600-dm mid-level ridge initially over the \nsouthern U.S. will flex northward in time. These above average \nheights remain a fixture in the forecast through Thursday. This \nsupports a return of warmer temperatures along with building \nhumidity levels. Daily highs push back into the mid/upper 80s, \nlocally reaching the low 90s in the warm spots. The return of \nthe humidity also favors milder nights with widespread lows in \nthe 60s. Any shower and thunderstorm chances should hold off \nuntil Thursday given the stout ridge aloft quelling most \nprecipitation chances. Most models keep such a threat mainly \nwest of the Shenandoah Valley, especially closer to the \nAlleghenies.\n\nA high latitude trough digging across southeastern Canada should\nbe enough to nudge the upper ridge back toward the south-central\nU.S. This allows a cold front to push through early Friday\nwhich again brings a gradual cool down with decreasing humidity\ninto Saturday. However, this reprieve is somewhat brief as \nadditional disturbances in the atmosphere track toward the area \nlate in the weekend. The overall jet pattern turns a bit more \ncomplex as an array of features enter the picture. A broad \nupper low retrogrades across the Gulf. At the same time, a \nmyriad of shortwaves will eject across the central U.S. toward \nthe Mississippi Valley. How these impulses behave along with \nwhere surface features reside will dictate any local weather \nimpacts. However, all signs point toward a more active pattern \non Sunday into early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /13Z TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nCanadian high pressure will support VFR conditions through mid-\nweek. Weak gradients keep lighter winds than yesterday/Monday,\nwith winds gradually shifting from east to southeasterly today.\nThis ridge eventually edges toward the Atlantic coastline which\nwill support mainly southerly winds on Wednesday into Thursday.\nSome storm chances emerge by Thursday, but these are currently\nexpected to stay west of the area TAF sites.\n\nA cold front moves through early Friday which results in some\nuptick in shower chances. This may come with a few restrictions\nif convection becomes more pronounced. In the wake of this\nfrontal passage, winds turn more northerly with afternoon gusts\nto around 15 knots or so. High pressure building to the north\nresults in an east to southeasterly wind for Saturday. This \ncomes with likely VFR conditions.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nGradients will continue to weaken through the day with winds \naround 5 to 10 knots.\n\nHigh pressure slowly pushes away toward the coast which results\nin a south to southeasterly wind for Wednesday and Thursday. \nSome southerly channeling effects may necessitate Small Craft \nAdvisories for parts of Thursday, especially across southern \nsections of the Chesapeake Bay. A cold front tracks across the \nwaters on Friday morning which favors a shift to north to \nnortheasterly winds. These could near advisory criteria, but \nconfidence is low. High pressure passing by to the north will \nresult in a shift to east-southeasterlies for Saturday. Gusts up\nto 15 to perhaps 20 knots are possible.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nCurrent tidal anomalies remain around zero feet. However, expect\na gradual rise in water levels later today as high pressure \npulls away from the Eastern Seaboard. This again pushes a number\nof sites into Action stage during the higher of the two \nastronomical high tides, particularly tonight and again late\nWednesday.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...BRO/DHOF\nAVIATION...BRO/DHOF\nMARINE...BRO/DHOF\nTIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...BRO\n"},"forecast":{"t":1789495874,"days":{"2026-09-15":{"hi":80,"lo":61,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-16":{"hi":87,"lo":67,"pop":4,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-09-17":{"hi":90,"lo":72,"pop":26,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-18":{"hi":86,"lo":67,"pop":12,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-09-19":{"hi":82,"lo":68,"pop":11,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-09-20":{"hi":88,"lo":68,"pop":40,"short":"Partly Sunny then Chance Showers And Thunderstorms"}